# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Corpus data. Never committed — see README "What lives where".
# Large artifacts go to Google Drive; links are in the README.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/
*.jsonl
*.jsonl.zst
*.zst
*.gz
*.bz2
*.xml
*.warc

# Raw scrape output and OCR intermediates
**/raw/
**/cache/
*.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored (these are graded and must be committed):
#   */tokenizer/*.model   SentencePiece model, ~1 MB
#   */tokenizer/*.vocab   vocabulary, plain text
#   */splits/val.ids      split manifests, small (~0.4-1.2 MB)
#   */splits/test.ids     split manifests, small (~0.4-1.2 MB)
#   */stats/*.json|*.csv  statistics
#   report/phase1_report.pdf  the graded report itself
# train.ids is NOT committed -- see rule below. It is regenerable (train =
# corpus minus val.ids minus test.ids) and goes to Drive alongside the
# corpus text instead, not into git history.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Report LaTeX source: kept locally for recompiling in Overleaf when the
# report needs an update, but not tracked -- the graded deliverable is the
# compiled report/phase1_report.pdf, not the source that produced it.
report/*.tex

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Local machine-specific launcher scripts (hardcoded paths, Task Scheduler wrappers)
scheduled/

# Local run logs (e.g. background collection/expansion runs) -- not deliverables
*.log

# Secrets / credentials
.env
kaggle.json
*.key

# OS / editor
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/

# Train-split id manifests: large at full corpus scale (Hindi 41.7MB,
# Assamese 20.5MB) and fully reproducible from the corpus + val/test ids
# (train = everything not in val.ids or test.ids), so not worth committing.
*/splits/train.ids

# Flat train-corpus text extracted for tokenizer training (5+ GB each) --
# the corpus text itself already goes to Drive, and this is regenerable
# from the corpus + train.ids via common/tokenizer.py's extract step.
*/tokenizer/train_corpus.txt

# Staged Kaggle upload payload: a copy of code that already lives in this repo,
# regenerable with `python kaggle/stage_code_payload.py`. Committing it would
# duplicate every module and let the copy drift from the original.
kaggle/code_payload/

# Model checkpoints: ~290 MB each, and the spec is explicit that large binary
# artifacts go to Google Drive with links in the README, never into git. The
# small companions next to them (config.json, parameter_report.json,
# train_log.jsonl, loss_curve_*.png) are graded and ARE committed.
*.pt
*.pth

# ...but the per-step training log is a graded deliverable ("training logs and
# loss curves in the repo"), not corpus data. It is a few hundred KB of JSON
# lines, so exempt it from the blanket *.jsonl rule above.
!*/model/train_log.jsonl
# ...and the ablation writes to model_nopos/, which the line above does not
# match. This blanket *.jsonl rule has now silently swallowed three separate
# graded logs (Phase 2 pretraining, Phase 3 finetuning, this one), so match the
# filename wherever it lives rather than adding a fourth directory-specific
# exception later.
!**/train_log.jsonl
# Same story for finetuning logs, which now exist in two places: the Phase 3
# runs and the per-size data-size ablation. Match the filename wherever it
# lives, for the same reason as the line above.
!**/finetune_log.jsonl

# Phase 3 reasoning data. val/test are small and graded -- they are the sets the
# accuracy numbers come from, so they are committed (see the negation below).
# The 19 MB train split is not: it is regenerable byte-for-byte from a fixed
# seed with `python -m common.reasoning.generate --lang <lang>`, exactly as
# Phase 1 treated train.ids.
*/train/reasoning_train.jsonl
!*/train/reasoning_val.jsonl
!*/train/reasoning_test.jsonl
!*/train/reasoning_test_unseen.jsonl

# The finetuning log is a graded Phase 3 deliverable ("Finetuning logs, metrics,
# qualitative examples"), and the blanket *.jsonl rule above would swallow it --
# the same way it nearly swallowed */model/train_log.jsonl in Phase 2. Negation
# rules match exact filenames, so this needs its own line.
!*/train/finetuned/finetune_log.jsonl
